/*! @brief TcpServer is a high-concurrency TCP Socket server that can be used to create a TCP server that has already established TCP connections with clients in the initial state

Using the `TcpServer` object, you can quickly create a multi-fiber concurrent TCP server. When a client connects to the listening address, the callback function will be called and return a new connected `Socket` object, which can be used to send or receive TCP packets to/from the client.

Below is a specific example of implementing a TCP server that echoes client TCP packets based on the `TcpServer` object:

```JavaScript
const net = require("net");

function onConnect(conn) {
  console.log(`new client accepted! local:${conn.localAddress}, remote:${conn.remoteAddress}`);
  const data = conn.read();
  if (data) {
    console.log(`recv data on fn onConnect: ${data}`);
    conn.write(data);
  }
  conn.close();
}

new net.TcpServer('0.0.0.0', 8080, onConnect).start();
console.log('server is running on port: 8080');
```
In the above code, we create a `TcpServer` object and use the callback function `onConnect` to process the received client request information, echoing the data back to the client.

When starting this service, it will listen on all IP addresses and requests on port `8080`. When you connect to this service using `telnet` or other client tools, you will see the service print connection information and send back every request you send as is.
 */
interface TcpServer : object
{
    /*! @brief TcpServer constructor, listening on all local addresses
    @param port specifies the TCP server listening port
    @param listener specifies the built-in message handler for received TCP, handler function, chain processing array, routing object, see mq.Handler for details
   */
    TcpServer(Integer port, Handler listener);

    /*! @brief TcpServer constructor
    @param addr specifies the TCP server listening address, "" means listen on all local addresses
    @param port specifies the TCP server listening port
    @param listener specifies the built-in message handler for received connections, handler function, chain processing array, routing object, see mq.Handler for details
   */
    TcpServer(String addr, Integer port, Handler listener);

    /*! @brief TcpServer constructor
    @param addr specifies the unix socket or Windows pipe server listening address
    @param listener specifies the built-in message handler for received connections, handler function, chain processing array, routing object, see mq.Handler for details
   */
    TcpServer(String addr, Handler listener);

    /*! @brief Start the current server */
    start();

    /*! @brief Close socket to stop the running server */
    stop() async;

    /*! @brief The Socket object currently listened by the server */
    readonly Socket socket;

    /*! @brief Query and set timeout time in milliseconds, this timeout is used to set newly received connections*/
    Integer timeout;

    /*! @brief Current event handler interface object of the server */
    Handler handler;
};